    "source": "process.stdout.write(\"   I want you to evaluate this project holistically and identify the highest-impact work that would make it more useful, reliable, polished, and valuable.\\n   Act as four roles at once:\\n   1. User — what feels broken, confusing, missing, slow, or untrustworthy?\\n   2. Product manager — what work most improves activation, retention, usefulness, clarity, or differentiation?\\n   3. Developer — what bugs, debt, brittle code, poor tests, or workflow problems are slowing progress?\\n   4. Architect — what structural risks, scaling limits, integration problems, or design choices will block the project soon?\\n   Use Claude Fable 5 behavior:\\n   - When you have enough information to act, act.\\n   - Do not over-plan or produce an exhaustive survey.\\n   - Do not re-derive facts already established by project files.\\n   - Recommend the highest-leverage work, not every possible improvement.\\n   - Prefer obvious, immediate value over speculative long-term architecture.\\n   - Ground every claim in project evidence: code, docs, issues, tests, UI behavior, logs, config, or repository history.\\n   - If something is an inference, label it as an inference.\\n   - Do not implement anything yet unless I explicitly ask.\\n   Your task:\\n   Review the project as a whole and produce a prioritized action plan of the most valuable work to do next.\\n   Look for:\\n   - Bugs that visibly harm the user experience.\\n   - Missing features that block the project’s core promise.\\n   - UX friction, confusing flows, weak copy, or unclear onboarding.\\n   - Reliability problems, crashes, broken states, or data-loss risks.\\n   - Performance issues that users would actually notice.\\n   - Security or privacy issues that create real risk.\\n   - Developer-experience problems that slow future work.\\n   - Test gaps around critical behavior.\\n   - Documentation gaps that block setup, usage, or contribution.\\n   - Architecture decisions that are already causing pain or will soon.\\n   - Incomplete tasks, stale TODOs, half-finished features, or abandoned scaffolding.\\n   - Existing issue/task tracker items that should be closed, promoted, merged, or deprioritized.\\n   Workflow:\\n   1. Orient yourself in the repository.\\n   2. Read the project’s README, docs, package/config files, task/issue tracker if present, and key application entry points.\\n   3. Inspect the main user flows or product surfaces.\\n   4. Review tests, build scripts, CI, and developer workflow.\\n   5. Search for signs of incomplete work, fragile behavior, TODOs, FIXME comments, dead code, failing tests, or stale docs.\\n   6. If the app can be run safely, smoke-test the most important user path.\\n   7. Synthesize findings across the four roles: user, product manager, developer, architect.\\n   8. Prioritize only the work with clear near-term value.\\n   Output:\\n   Start with the outcome: the top 3 things this project should do next.\\n   Then provide:\\n   ## Executive recommendation\\n   A short plain-English summary of what would most improve the project right now and why.\\n   ## Top priorities\\n   For each priority, include:\\n   - Title\\n   - Category: bug, feature, UX, reliability, performance, security, test, docs, developer experience, architecture, or cleanup\\n   - Impact: high / medium / low\\n   - Effort: small / medium / large\\n   - Confidence: high / medium / low\\n   - Why it matters\\n   - Evidence from the project\\n   - User-visible value\\n   - Suggested implementation approach\\n   - Verification method\\n   - Risks or tradeoffs\\n   - Whether it should be done now, later, or not at all\\n   ## Ranked roadmap\\n   Group recommendations into:\\n   ### Must do now\\n   Only items with immediate, apparent value or serious risk reduction.\\n   ### Do next\\n   Useful work that compounds value after the must-do items.\\n   ### Worth considering\\n   Good ideas, but not urgent or not yet proven.\\n   ### Do not prioritize\\n   Tempting work that is low impact, premature, too speculative, or mostly cosmetic.\\n   ## Quick wins\\n   List small fixes that can be completed quickly and would noticeably improve quality or usability.\\n   ## Strategic bets\\n   List larger features or architectural changes only if they clearly support the project’s core purpose.\\n   ## Things to stop doing\\n   Identify work, complexity, abstractions, features, or processes that appear to waste time or dilute the product.\\n   ## Evidence reviewed\\n   List the files, docs, commands, tests, UI paths, issues, or logs you inspected.\\n   Boundaries:\\n   - Do not implement fixes yet.\\n   - Do not create new files unless needed for notes during analysis.\\n   - Do not make destructive changes.\\n   - Do not invent user needs unsupported by the project.\\n   - Do not recommend broad rewrites unless the evidence clearly supports them.\\n   - Do not prioritize internal cleanup above user-visible value unless it blocks delivery or causes real risk.\\n   - Do not propose generic best practices unless they directly apply here.\\n   - Do not ask me what to do next if the repository gives enough evidence to recommend a path.\\n   Self-verification before final answer:\\n   Before reporting progress or recommendations, audit each claim against evidence from this session. If a claim is not directly verified, label it as an inference. If tests fail, commands fail,\\n docs are stale, or the app cannot run, report that plainly and include the relevant evidence.\\n   Final deliverable:\\n   A concise, evidence-grounded project improvement plan that tells me what to do next, why it matters, and how to verify success.\");"
